[THOUGHT] Social history is negative for smoking and relevant exposures; occupation involves visual strain but no physical triggers — consistent with symptom fluctuation being driven by neuromuscular fatigue rather than mechanical or environmental factors. Next is **Domain 7: Targeted physical examination**, which must include assessment for fatigable signs: ptosis (though patient denies it, it must be objectively checked), extraocular motility (to identify incomitant diplopia and cranial nerve involvement), sustained upward gaze test, and proximal muscle strength testing (e.g., shoulder abduction, hip flexion) before and after brief exertion to demonstrate fatigability. Since I cannot perform the exam directly, I must request that these specific maneuvers be carried out.

[TEST] Please perform and report the following targeted physical examination:  
- Observe for resting ptosis (upper eyelid position) and repeat after 30 seconds of sustained upward gaze;  
- Test extraocular movements in all nine cardinal positions — note any restriction, nystagmus, or induction of diplopia;  
- Assess shoulder abduction strength bilaterally at baseline, then retest after 30 seconds of sustained arm elevation at 90 degrees;  
- Assess hip flexion strength bilaterally at baseline, then retest after 30 seconds of sitting with knees extended and attempting to lift one leg against resistance.

